{% doc %}
  Renders a full row of swatches for one product option.

  `swatch.liquid` draws a single value; this draws the option — the inputs, the
  labels, the overflow control and the availability marking — so a section never
  has to assemble that itself.

  ## Where a swatch comes from

  Four sources, narrowest first. Each one exists because the one after it is not
  good enough for someone:

  1. **Shopify's native swatch** on the option value. Per-product, survives a
     theme change, and is what Shopify itself uses in filters and search.
  2. **A variant metafield** (`variant.metafields.custom.swatch_image`). This is
     the escape hatch for a pattern that is genuinely an image — marble, wood
     grain, a printed fabric — where a hex code is a lie.
  3. **The theme's swatch map**, `settings.swatch_config`. The practical option
     when option values are shared across a whole catalogue.
  4. **The fallback**, chosen in settings.

  Dual-tone values are supported by writing two hex codes separated by a slash in
  the map — `Navy/White=#292B6C/#ffffff` — because a two-colour product rendered
  as one colour is wrong in a way customers notice immediately.

  ## Why unavailable values are marked and not removed

  Hiding a sold-out size tells the customer nothing. A disabled radio cannot be
  focused, so a keyboard user can no longer discover it exists. Both are worse
  than a struck-through swatch with a title saying why.

  The control markup lives in `swatch-input.liquid` and the visual in
  `swatch.liquid`. This snippet owns only the row: the loop, the availability
  test, and the overflow control. Three files rather than one, because the same
  visual has to appear in a filter checkbox and on a product card where neither
  a radio nor a row is wanted.

  Parameters:
    product (product, required)
    option  (object, required)   From `product.options_with_values`
    section (object, required)
    max_visible (number, optional) 0 for no limit
    shape, size (string, optional) Passed through

  @example
    {% render 'advanced-swatches', product: product, option: option, section: section %}
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign limit = max_visible | default: settings.swatch_max_visible | default: 0
  assign shape = shape | default: settings.swatch_shape | default: 'circle'
  assign size = size | default: settings.swatch_size_pdp | default: 'medium'
-%}

<variant-swatches
  class="advanced-swatches advanced-swatches--{{ shape }}"
  data-option="{{ option.name | escape }}"
  data-max-visible="{{ limit }}"
  data-trigger="{{ settings.swatch_trigger | default: 'click' }}"
>
  <div class="advanced-swatches__list">
    {%- for value in option.values -%}
      {%- liquid
        assign input_id = section.id | append: '-swatch-' | append: option.position | append: '-' | append: forloop.index

        comment
          Availability is evaluated against this value alone, which is enough for
          the row. `<variant-picker>` re-evaluates it against the other selected
          options as soon as it connects.
        endcomment
        assign matching = false
        for variant in product.variants
          if variant.options contains value and variant.available
            assign matching = true
            break
          endif
        endfor

        assign variant_for_value = product.variants | where: 'title', value | first
      -%}

      {% render 'swatch-input',
         value: value,
         option: option,
         section_id: section.id,
         index: forloop.index,
         checked: option.selected_value == value,
         available: matching,
         variant: variant_for_value,
         shape: shape,
         size: size %}
    {%- endfor -%}
  </div>

  {%- comment -%}
    Rendered always and revealed by the element, because whether the row overflows
    depends on the limit and on how many values exist — neither of which Liquid
    should duplicate the arithmetic for.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <button type="button" class="advanced-swatches__more button button--link" data-ref="more" hidden></button>
</variant-swatches>
